            {
              "id": "a6130101-ac1d-46d7-8d86-0832c1982244",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "<em><strong>Playthings in Early Modernity: Party Games, Word Games, Mind Games </strong></em>is a collection of essays written by Allison Levy, published by Medieval Institute Publications, February 22, 2017."
            },
            {
              "id": "1086b1d1-8d37-48b4-9cc4-67c442f1666f",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            },
            {
              "id": "33a043da-b9d6-4634-ba62-842e6aa0c0d3",
              "type": "quote",
              "html": "<em>An innovative volume of fifteen interdisciplinary essays at the nexus of material culture, performance studies, and game theory, </em>Playthings in Early Modernity <em>emphasizes the rules of the game(s) as well as the breaking of those rules. Thus, the titular &quot;&quot;plaything&quot;&quot; is understood as both an object and a person, and play, in the early modern world, is treated not merely as a pastime, a leisurely pursuit, but as a pivotal part of daily life, a strategic psychosocial endeavor.</em>"
            }
          ]
        },
